---
import ExampleShowcase from './ExampleShowcase.astro';
import StarterSetup from './StarterSetup.astro';
const base = '/raveldex/';
---
<ExampleShowcase pilot family="dropdown" title="Compact header search starter" description="Find a page from a compact header field, with a complete results page when you need more." previewTitle="Dropdown header starter preview" previewHeight={680}>
  <Fragment slot="preview-hint">Search <code>walking guide</code>. Use the arrow keys and Enter, or choose <strong>See all results</strong>.</Fragment>
  <h2 id="copy-dropdown">Copy the complete project</h2>
  <StarterSetup family="dropdown" pilot />
  <p>The quick popup needs native Popover. Otherwise, use the header form to open the working full results page.</p>
  <details><summary>Data and browser requirements</summary>
    <p>Install Raveldex and the pinned esbuild tooling using the setup guide. This title/link example uses core directly; it needs no snippet, facet, section, worker or storage registration. Native Popover support is required for the header popup.</p>
  </details>
  <details><summary>How this example works</summary>
<p>Put a search field in your header, show a few matching pages and link to a complete results page. Copy the browser project, replace its public guide records and adjust the scoped CSS.</p>
  <p>Choose this starter for a compact title-and-link search. Use <a href={base + 'examples/inline/'}>inline search</a> for results that stay in the page, or the <a href={base + 'examples/modal/'}>documentation modal</a> for search opened from a button with anchored section previews.</p>
<p>Search <code>walking guide</code>: seven pages match and the header shows up to three. Use the arrow keys to select a result and Enter to follow its actual link, or choose <strong>See all results</strong> to open the inline results page. Load more appends the remaining pages while keeping earlier results displayed. Escape dismisses the popup; Tab leaves it normally.</p>
  </details>
  <h2 id="dropdown-theme">Change the appearance</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Copy, install, build and preview the complete project using its guide. Search the sample records before changing them.</li>
    <li>Edit the existing <code>.raveldex-dropdown</code> rule in <code>theme.css</code>, loaded after the package styles. Change the accent, corners and spacing:</li>
  </ol>
  <pre role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Dropdown search theme properties"><code>{`.raveldex-dropdown {
  --raveldex-dropdown-accent: #145b45;
  --raveldex-dropdown-radius: .75rem;
  --raveldex-dropdown-gap: .75rem;
}`}</code></pre>
  <ol start="3">
    <li>Stop the preview, run <code>npm run build</code> in the copied project and restart its documented preview command with the same deployment base. Reload to see the green accent, rounder corners and wider spacing.</li>
    <li>Search <code>walking guide</code> again. Seven pages still match; the compact window still shows up to three. Follow See all results, check the unchanged query, load the other pages and follow a real guide link. Use Tab to inspect visible focus.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Set the background, text, muted, border, focus and active-result colors together for dark themes. The full-results page uses <code>--raveldex-inline-*</code> properties. See <a href={base + 'reference/hosts/#compact-dropdown-search'}>dropdown options and styling</a>.</p>
  <h2 id="dropdown-narrow">Narrow headers and an optional larger search</h2>
  <p>The native top-layer popup can escape an overflow-clipped header. Controls wrap at narrow widths. When the viewport cannot fit a usable result row, the exact total and full-results link stay available; this does not turn into a false no-results state. Nothing automatically opens another search surface.</p>
  <p>For an explicit larger-search button, set <code>modalHandoff: true</code> in <code>configuration.mjs</code>, rebuild and use <strong>Open expanded search</strong>. It transfers the current header query into the existing native modal, sharing the page's owner, records and prefix mode. It adds no competing global shortcut or second engine. The default is <code>false</code>. The supplied build includes the modal code and explicit stylesheet so this switch can be tried without changing imports; its setup guide explains that cost and how the surfaces share ownership.</p>
  <p>Keep the complete results route and browse links usable before loading, after a loading failure and without JavaScript. Rebuild and deploy the pages, content and assets together when changing the base or records.</p>
  <h2 id="dropdown-support">Before using your own theme</h2>
  <p>Installed-consumer checks and the acceptance record describe the tested browser, lifecycle and emulated layout scope. Human screen-reader speech, physical IME/touch/on-screen keyboards, actual browser zoom and other browser/device combinations need separate checks in your application. See <a href={base + 'reference/hosts/#starter-adoption-and-local-checks'}>starter adoption and local checks</a>. Playwright WebKit observations do not establish Safari support.</p>
</ExampleShowcase>
